Every function in JavaScript keeps an internal link to the scope chain that was active where it was **defined** — not where it's called. Normally that link is invisible, because the outer scope disappears once the outer function returns.

A closure is what happens when the inner function **outlives** the outer one: as long as any reference to the inner function still exists, the JavaScript engine keeps the outer variables it touches alive too, instead of discarding them at return.

```javascript
function makeCounter() {
  let count = 0;
  return function () {
    count += 1;
    return count;
  };
}
const counter = makeCounter();
counter(); // 1
counter(); // 2
```

`makeCounter` returns and its stack frame is gone, but `count` is still alive — pinned in memory because the returned function still references it. Each call to `makeCounter()` creates a **new**, independent `count`.

Before ES6 classes and `#private` fields, closures were the standard way to get truly private state — a variable no outside code can read or write except through functions you expose:

```javascript
function createBankAccount(initialBalance) {
  let balance = initialBalance; // unreachable from outside

  return {
    deposit(amount) { balance += amount; return balance; },
    withdraw(amount) {
      if (amount > balance) throw new Error("insufficient funds");
      balance -= amount;
      return balance;
    },
    getBalance() { return balance; },
  };
}

const acct = createBankAccount(100);
acct.deposit(50);   // 150
acct.balance;        // undefined — there is no such property
```

There is no `this.balance` anywhere, so there's nothing to accidentally overwrite from outside and no property to enumerate in a `for...in`. This is the **module pattern**, and it's still how a lot of real library internals hide state today, class fields or not.

A closure can hold a cache the same way it holds any other variable — alive across calls, invisible from outside:

```javascript
function memoize(fn) {
  const cache = new Map();
  return function (...args) {
    const key = JSON.stringify(args);
    if (cache.has(key)) return cache.get(key);
    const result = fn(...args);
    cache.set(key, result);
    return result;
  };
}

const slowSquare = (n) => { for (let i = 0; i < 1e8; i++); return n * n; };
const fastSquare = memoize(slowSquare);
fastSquare(5); // slow the first time
fastSquare(5); // instant — read from cache
```

`cache` is invisible to any caller of `fastSquare` — there's no way to inspect or corrupt it except through the function itself. This is the same shape as the bank account: a closure turns a plain variable into state that persists across calls without ever becoming a public property.

This is the single most-asked closures question, and it's a direct consequence of `var` being function-scoped rather than block-scoped:

```javascript
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100);
}
// logs: 3, 3, 3 — not 0, 1, 2
```

All three arrow functions close over the **same** `i`, because `var` creates exactly one binding for the whole loop. By the time any `setTimeout` callback actually runs — well after the synchronous loop has already finished — `i` holds its final value, `3`, and every closure reads that same shared variable.

The mistake candidates make when explaining this: saying "it's because `setTimeout` is async." Async timing is why the callbacks run *late*, but it's the **shared variable**, not the lateness, that makes them all print the same number.

All three fixes solve the same problem — give each iteration its own variable instead of sharing one — using different mechanisms:

```javascript
// 1. let — the modern default, one line changed
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 100);
} // 0, 1, 2 — a fresh binding of i per iteration

// 2. IIFE — the pre-ES6 fix, manually creating a new scope
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  (function (iCopy) {
    setTimeout(() => console.log(iCopy), 100);
  })(i);
} // 0, 1, 2 — iCopy is a fresh parameter per call

// 3. Explicit binding via an extra argument to setTimeout
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout((iCopy) => console.log(iCopy), 100, i);
} // 0, 1, 2 — i is captured by value at the setTimeout call
```

All three work by giving the closure its **own** copy of the loop variable instead of a shared reference to one. `let` does it implicitly via per-iteration bindings; the other two do it explicitly by passing `i` as a fresh parameter.

The same mechanism that makes closures useful makes them a real memory-leak source: a closure keeps **everything in its lexical scope** reachable, not just the variables it actually uses.

```javascript
function setup() {
  const hugeData = new Array(1_000_000).fill("x"); // several MB
  const smallHandler = () => console.log("clicked");
  document.addEventListener("click", smallHandler);
}
```

Depending on the engine, `smallHandler`'s closure can pin the **entire surrounding scope**, including `hugeData`, even though the handler never reads it — because both live in the same function's variable environment and V8 historically kept that environment as one object. The fix is to scope large data separately from the function you actually keep a long-lived reference to, or null it out once you're done, so nothing keeps the whole environment reachable.

A closure captures the **variable**, not a snapshot of its value at creation time. This is the same fact underlying both the loop bug and the counter's mutability — it's worth stating on its own because it trips people up outside loops too.

```javascript
function outer() {
  let value = "first";
  const readValue = () => value;
  value = "second";
  return readValue;
}
outer()(); // "second", not "first"
```

`readValue` doesn't remember `"first"` — it holds a live pointer to the `value` binding itself, and reads whatever is there when it's finally called. This is why closures can implement mutable state (counters, caches) at all: if they captured values instead of references, every closure would be permanently frozen at creation.

"Write a counter/debounce function using closures" is a real, common prompt, not just a definition check. A debounce is the sharper version because it makes you close over **two** pieces of state:

```javascript
function debounce(fn, delayMs) {
  let timerId; // closed over, persists across calls
  return function (...args) {
    clearTimeout(timerId);
    timerId = setTimeout(() => fn.apply(this, args), delayMs);
  };
}
const onResize = debounce(() => console.log("resized"), 200);
window.addEventListener("resize", onResize);
```

`timerId` has to survive between calls to cancel the *previous* pending timer — that's the closure doing exactly what it does for a counter, just holding a timer handle instead of a number. Recognizing that "needs to remember something between calls, without a global" is always a closure signals fluency faster than reciting the definition.
